This puts this concept in a bit of a catch-22 then. The prior PR had concerns of "locking in" the `Variant` casts, while this has the concern of changing behavior. I absolutely agree with the more egregious cases deserving a deeper-dive in their own right, but I don't know how much of a blocker that is relative to this scenario

Regarding the behavior change: I think that might be the bigger issue. Ideally, the operations constrained by `Variant` should reflect the operations against the types they're scoping; that is:
```cpp
TEST_CASE("[Variant] Complex equality") {
	CHECK_EQ(123 == 123.0, Variant(123) == Variant(123.0));
	CHECK_EQ(StringName("A") == String("A"), Variant(StringName("A")) == Variant(String("A")));
	CHECK_EQ(Vector2() == Vector2i(), Variant(Vector2()) == Variant(Vector2i()));
}
```
The logical thing would be for every case here to pass, but actually results in `CHECK_EQ(true, false)` for each. This was meant to be more of a demonstration, but seeing this play out honestly rubber duck'd me into realizing how pertinent this actually is, so I'll be shifting gears to handling this now
